  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Where does mains water pressure come from?",
      "a": "From height and pumps. Water is stored in high reservoirs, water towers or pumped mains, and the weight of water above, or the pump, pushes water through the pipes to every house.",
      "asks": [
        "How does water get upstairs without a pump?",
        "Why is tap water under pressure?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the stopcock for?",
      "a": "The stopcock is the main valve where the water supply enters your home. Turning it off stops all the mains water, which you need to do quickly if a pipe bursts.",
      "asks": [
        "Where is the main water valve?",
        "How do I turn off the water to my house?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is my upstairs shower weak?",
      "a": "If it is fed from a tank in the loft, the tank is only a short way above the shower, so the pressure is small. Higher-up taps also have less pressure than lower ones on the same supply.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is the water pressure low upstairs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the tank in the loft for?",
      "a": "In many older homes, mains water fills a cold-water tank in the loft, which then feeds the hot-water cylinder and some taps by gravity. It keeps a store of water if the mains is cut off.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do some houses have a water tank in the attic?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is water pressure lower at the top of a hill?",
      "a": "Pressure depends on how far the water source is above you. A house high on a hill is closer to the reservoir level, so less height is left to push the water.",
      "asks": [
        "Does height affect water pressure?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Every 10 metres of height of water adds about 1 bar of pressure.",
    "The stopcock is the valve where the mains water enters a home and can shut off the whole supply."
  ],
